@startuml tier1-lock-acquire
' Tier-1 single-owner advisory-lock acquisition at a DiskManager open/create chokepoint.
' Rendered by scripts/render-diagrams.sh → ../tier1-lock-acquire.svg
skinparam backgroundColor #FDFDFD
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName "DejaVu Sans"
skinparam ArrowColor #5F6368
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #E8F0FE
  BorderColor #5F6368
  DiamondBackgroundColor #FEF7E0
  DiamondBorderColor #E0C060
  StartColor #5F6368
  EndColor #5F6368
}
title Tier-1 — acquire_exclusive_lock (once per OPEN, never per operation)

start
:DiskManager::{create|open|open_without_validation};
:key ← canonicalize(path)\n(parent dir + filename if the file does not exist yet);

partition "process-global lock registry (Mutex<HashMap>)" {
  if (key already present?\n(same process already owns it)) then (yes)
    :entry.refs += 1;
    :return WLockGuard\n(SHARES the existing flock — e.g. reopen\nor a crash-sim mem::forget'd owner);<<#CEEAD6>>
    kill
  else (no)
    :open "<path>.wlock" sidecar;
    if (flock(LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB)) then (EWOULDBLOCK\n— another PROCESS holds it)
      :return Err(FileLocked { path });<<#F6AEA9>>
      kill
    else (Ok)
      :registry.insert(key → { file, refs = 1 });
      :return WLockGuard (new flock);<<#CEEAD6>>
      kill
    endif
  endif
}

note right
  The returned guard is stored in the DiskManager for its whole lifetime.
  Dropping the LAST guard for a path removes the registry entry, which drops
  the `.wlock` File and releases the OS `flock`. A `mem::forget` leaks a
  refcount, so the lock stays held until the process exits — exactly a crash.

  This runs ONCE per open. The lock-free CAS write path and wait-free read
  path take NO lock — the single-process non-blocking invariant is preserved.
end note
@enduml
